/* =====================================================
   FUGA MYTHICA — ROOT_STORIES for German, batch 2: more native stems + affixes
   ===================================================== */
Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.de, {
  sitz: ["sitzen(坐)来自原始日耳曼语 *sitjaną,印欧语 *sed-——与拉丁语 sedere、希腊语 hezomai、英语 sit、俄语 sidet' 同根;setzen(放)是它的使役式(\"使坐\",英语 set),Sattel(鞍)、Sessel(扶手椅)、Nest(\"坐下处\")同源。Sitz 座位、总部,Sitz-ung 会议(session 的直译),be-sitz-en 拥有(\"坐在上面\"——possess 的同构:pot-sidere),Be-sitz 财产,Be-sitz-er 主人,Vor-sitz 主席职,Vor-sitz-ender 主席(\"坐在前面的\"——praeses),Ab-satz 段落、鞋跟、销售(\"放下的\"),An-satz 开端、方法,Auf-satz 作文,Ein-satz 投入、使命,Er-satz 替代(\"补放的\"——ersatz 进了英语),Ge-setz 法律(\"定下的\"——statute 同构),Satz 句子、乐章、定理,Über-setz-ung 翻译(\"摆渡过去\"——translatio 的直译),Um-satz 营业额,Vor-satz 意图,Wider-setz-lich 违抗,Zu-satz 添加,Ge-sess-en 坐过,be-setz-en 占领,Be-satz-ung 占领军、机组。",
    "sitzen (to sit) comes from Proto-Germanic *sitjaną, PIE *sed- — kin to Latin sedere, Greek hezomai, English sit, Russian sidet'; setzen (to set) is its causative (to make sit; English set), and Sattel (saddle), Sessel (armchair), Nest (the sitting-down place) are cognate. Sitz a seat, a headquarters, Sitz-ung a session, be-sitz-en to own (to sit upon — the build of possess, pot-sidere), Be-sitz property, Be-sitz-er an owner, Vor-sitz the chair, Vor-sitz-ender a chairman (praeses, sitting in front), Ab-satz a paragraph, a heel, sales (what is set down), An-satz an approach, Auf-satz an essay, Ein-satz commitment, a mission, Er-satz a substitute (set in place — ersatz entered English), Ge-setz a law (what is set — the build of statute), Satz a sentence, a movement, a theorem, Über-setz-ung translation (a ferrying across — a calque of translatio), Um-satz turnover, Vor-satz intention, Zu-satz an addition, be-setz-en to occupy, Be-satz-ung an occupying force, a crew."],
  leb: ["leben(活)来自原始日耳曼语 *libēną(留下、活着),印欧语 *leip-(粘、留)——与英语 live、life、leave(留下)、德语 bleiben(be-leiben 留下)、希腊语 lipos(脂肪——粘的)、梵语 limpati(涂)同根:活着就是\"留在世上\"。Leb-en 生命,leb-end-ig 活的,Leb-ens-lauf 简历(\"生命的进程\"——curriculum vitae 的直译),Leb-ens-mittel 食品(\"活命的手段\"),Leb-ens-lage 处境,er-leb-en 经历,Er-leb-nis 体验,über-leb-en 幸存,Über-leb-ende 幸存者,be-leb-en 使活跃,be-leb-t 热闹的,ver-leb-en 度过,Leb-ewesen 生物,Leb-er 肝(\"生命之脏\"——古人以为肝造血),Leb-kuchen 姜饼(词源不明),Leb-e-wohl 再见(\"活得好\"),leb-haft 活泼,Leb-ens-ge-fahr 生命危险,Leib 身体(古义生命——\"活体\";英语 life 的对应),Leib-wächter 保镖。",
    "leben (to live) comes from Proto-Germanic *libēną (to remain, to be alive), PIE *leip- (to stick, remain) — kin to English live, life, leave (to let remain), German bleiben (be-leiben, to stay), Greek lipos (fat — the sticky stuff), Sanskrit limpati (smears): to live is to remain in the world. Leb-en life, leb-end-ig alive, Leb-ens-lauf a CV (the course of a life — a calque of curriculum vitae), Leb-ens-mittel food (means of living), Leb-ens-lage a situation, er-leb-en to experience, Er-leb-nis an experience, über-leb-en to survive, Über-leb-ende a survivor, be-leb-en to enliven, be-leb-t busy, ver-leb-en to spend (time), Leb-ewesen a living being, Leb-er the liver (the organ of life — once thought to make blood), Leb-e-wohl farewell (live well), leb-haft lively, Leb-ens-ge-fahr mortal danger, Leib the body (once life itself — the living thing; English life), Leib-wächter a bodyguard."],
  groß: ["groß(大)来自西日耳曼语 *grautaz(粗、大),与英语 great(古义粗大)、荷兰语 groot、古弗里西亚语 grāt 同根;印欧语 *ghreu-(磨碎)存疑——\"粗颗粒的\"→大;拉丁语 grossus(粗、厚)是同义的另一个词(gross、grocer 从它来),两者可能互相影响。Größ-e 大小、伟大,Groß-vater 祖父(法语 grand-père 的直译,16 世纪),Groß-stadt 大城市,groß-artig 宏伟,Groß-teil 大部分,ver-größ-ern 放大,Ver-größ-erung 放大,groß-zügig 慷慨(\"大手笔\"),Groß-mut 宽宏,groß-ziehen 抚养,Groß-macht 大国,Groß-britannien 大不列颠,Groß-handel 批发,groß-schreiben 大写(德语名词大写是 16 世纪的习惯),Größen-wahn 妄自尊大,Groß-eltern 祖父母,groß-tun 吹嘘。",
    "groß (big) comes from West Germanic *grautaz (coarse, big), kin to English great (once coarse, thick), Dutch groot, Old Frisian grāt; PIE *ghreu- (to grind) is possible — coarse-grained → large; Latin grossus (thick) is a separate word of the same sense (gross, grocer), and the two may have coloured each other. Größ-e size, greatness, Groß-vater grandfather (a 16th-century calque of French grand-père), Groß-stadt a big city, groß-artig magnificent, Groß-teil the greater part, ver-größ-ern to enlarge, Ver-größ-erung an enlargement, groß-zügig generous (drawing large), Groß-mut magnanimity, groß-ziehen to bring up, Groß-macht a great power, Groß-britannien Great Britain, Groß-handel wholesale, groß-schreiben to capitalise (German capitalised its nouns from the 16th century), Größen-wahn megalomania, Groß-eltern grandparents, groß-tun to show off."],
  alt: ["alt(老)来自原始日耳曼语 *aldaz,是 *alaną(养育)的分词——\"长成的\";印欧语 *h₂el-(养),与拉丁语 altus(高——同样是\"长高了的\")、alere、adultus、英语 old、elder、荷兰语 oud 同根:老就是长足了年岁。Alt-er 年龄、老年,Alt-ers-heim 养老院,Alt-ertum 古代(\"老的时代\"——antiquitas),alt-modisch 老式,Alt-stadt 老城,Elt-ern 父母(\"较老的两位\"——比较级 elder!),ur-alt 极老,ver-alt-et 过时,alt-hergebracht 传统的,Alt-e 老人,Alt-meister 老前辈、大师,Alt-lasten 遗留问题,Welt 世界(wer-alt\"人的时代\"——world 同构),Alt-papier 废纸,Alt-bau 老建筑,Ält-ester 长老,alt-eingesessen 世居的。",
    "alt (old) comes from Proto-Germanic *aldaz, the participle of *alaną (to nourish) — grown up; PIE *h₂el- (to nourish), kin to Latin altus (high — likewise grown tall), alere, adultus, English old and elder, Dutch oud: old is full-grown in years. Alt-er age, old age, Alt-ers-heim an old people's home, Alt-ertum antiquity (the old time — antiquitas), alt-modisch old-fashioned, Alt-stadt the old town, Elt-ern parents (the two elder ones — the comparative elder!), ur-alt ancient, ver-alt-et obsolete, alt-hergebracht time-honoured, Alt-e the old, Alt-meister an old master, Alt-lasten legacy burdens, Welt the world (wer-alt, the age of man — the same build as world), Alt-papier waste paper, Alt-bau an old building, Ält-ester an elder, alt-eingesessen long-established."],
  neu: ["neu(新)来自原始日耳曼语 *niwjaz,印欧语 *newo-——与拉丁语 novus、希腊语 neos、梵语 nava、英语 new、俄语 novyj 同一个词。Neu-heit 新奇,Neu-ig-keit 新闻(\"新事\"),neu-lich 最近,er-neu-ern 更新,Er-neu-erung 革新,Er-neu-er-bar 可再生(erneuerbare Energie),neu-gierig 好奇(\"贪新的\"——Gier 贪),Neu-gier 好奇心,Neu-ling 新手,Neu-jahr 新年,Neu-bau 新建筑,Neu-zeit 近代(\"新时代\"——与 Altertum、Mittelalter 三分),neu-erdings 近来,Neu-mond 新月,Neu-ordnung 重组,Neu-anfang 新开始,neu-artig 新型的,Neu-er-ung 创新,Neu-seeland 新西兰(荷兰语 Zeeland),Neu-schnee 新雪,er-neu-t 再次。",
    "neu (new) comes from Proto-Germanic *niwjaz, PIE *newo- — one word with Latin novus, Greek neos, Sanskrit nava, English new, Russian novyj. Neu-heit novelty, Neu-ig-keit a piece of news (a new thing), neu-lich recently, er-neu-ern to renew, Er-neu-erung renewal, er-neu-er-bar renewable (erneuerbare Energie), neu-gierig curious (greedy for the new — Gier greed), Neu-gier curiosity, Neu-ling a novice, Neu-jahr New Year, Neu-bau a new building, Neu-zeit the modern era (the new time — after Altertum and Mittelalter), neu-erdings lately, Neu-mond new moon, Neu-ordnung reorganisation, Neu-anfang a fresh start, neu-artig novel, Neu-er-ung an innovation, Neu-seeland New Zealand (Dutch Zeeland), Neu-schnee fresh snow, er-neu-t once more."],
  krank: ["krank(病)在中古高地德语里先是\"弱、细\"(kranc),16 世纪才取代 siech(英语 sick 的对应,残存于 Siechtum、Seuche)成为\"生病\";词源可能与 kringen(弯曲)、英语 crank(弯柄)、cringe(蜷缩)同根——病是蜷缩、弯折。Krank-heit 疾病,Krank-en-haus 医院(\"病人之屋\"),Krank-en-wagen 救护车,Krank-en-kasse 医保(\"病人钱柜\"——俾斯麦 1883 年的创制),Krank-en-schwester 护士(\"病人的姐妹\"),Krank-en-pfleger 男护士,er-krank-en 患病,Er-krank-ung 疾病,kränk-en 伤人心(\"使病\"),Kränk-ung 冒犯,kränk-lich 多病,krank-schreib-en 开病假条,Krank-meld-ung 病假,See-krank 晕船,Heim-weh-krank 想家,Seuch-e 瘟疫。",
    "krank (ill) in Middle High German first meant weak, slender (kranc), and only in the 16th century replaced siech (the counterpart of English sick, surviving in Siechtum, Seuche); the root may be kin to kringen (to bend), English crank and cringe — illness as buckling. Krank-heit an illness, Krank-en-haus a hospital (the house of the sick), Krank-en-wagen an ambulance, Krank-en-kasse health insurance (the sick-fund — Bismarck's creation of 1883), Krank-en-schwester a nurse (the sick's sister), Krank-en-pfleger a male nurse, er-krank-en to fall ill, Er-krank-ung a disease, kränk-en to hurt someone's feelings (to make sick), Kränk-ung an affront, kränk-lich sickly, krank-schreib-en to sign off sick, Krank-meld-ung sick leave, see-krank seasick, Seuch-e a plague."],
  reis: ["Reise(旅行)来自原始日耳曼语 *raisō(起身、出发),是 *rīsaną(升起——英语 rise、raise、德语 reisen 古义\"起身\")的名词;古高地德语 reisa 是出征,中古高地德语指旅程。Reis-e 旅行,reis-en 旅行,Reis-ende 旅客,Reise-büro 旅行社,Reise-pass 护照,Reise-führer 导游、旅行指南,ab-reis-en 出发,Ab-reis-e 出发,an-reis-en 抵达,An-reis-e 到达,ver-reis-en 外出,Reise-ziel 目的地,Dienst-reis-e 出差,Reise-kosten 差旅费,Ge-schäfts-reise 商务旅行,Reise-fieber 旅行前的兴奋,Reis(米)是另一个词(拉丁语 oryza)。英语 raise、rise 是它的日耳曼亲戚。",
    "Reise (a journey) comes from Proto-Germanic *raisō (a rising, a setting-out), the noun of *rīsaną (to rise — English rise, raise; German reisen once meant to get up); Old High German reisa was a military expedition, Middle High German a journey. Reis-e a journey, reis-en to travel, Reis-ende a traveller, Reise-büro a travel agency, Reise-pass a passport, Reise-führer a guide, a guidebook, ab-reis-en to depart, Ab-reis-e departure, an-reis-en to arrive, An-reis-e arrival, ver-reis-en to go away, Reise-ziel a destination, Dienst-reis-e a business trip, Reise-kosten travel expenses, Ge-schäfts-reise a business journey, Reise-fieber pre-trip excitement; Reis (rice) is another word (Latin oryza). English raise and rise are its Germanic cousins."],
  ruh: ["Ruhe(安静)来自原始日耳曼语 *rōwō(休息),与英语 row?否——与古英语 rōw(安宁)、荷兰语 rust、古挪威语 ró 同根;印欧语 *reh₁-(休息),与希腊语 erōē(停止)同源。ruh-ig 安静的,be-ruh-ig-en 安抚,Be-ruh-ig-ung 安慰,Be-ruh-ig-ungs-mittel 镇静剂,be-ruh-en 基于(\"安放在…上\"),Ruhe-stand 退休(\"安息的状态\"),Ruhe-tag 休息日,un-ruh-ig 不安,Un-ruh-e 骚乱,Ruhe-pause 休息,Ruhe-störung 扰民,ruh-en 休息(拉丁语 requiescat 的译词:Ruhe in Frieden),aus-ruh-en 歇息,Ruhe-los 不安宁,Ge-ruh-sam 悠闲,Ruh-e-los-igkeit 焦躁,Rast 是另一个词(rest)。",
    "Ruhe (rest, quiet) comes from Proto-Germanic *rōwō (rest), kin to Old English rōw (calm), Dutch rust, Old Norse ró; PIE *reh₁- (to rest), with Greek erōē (a ceasing). ruh-ig quiet, be-ruh-ig-en to calm, Be-ruh-ig-ung reassurance, Be-ruh-ig-ungs-mittel a sedative, be-ruh-en to rest on, Ruhe-stand retirement (the state of rest), Ruhe-tag a day off, un-ruh-ig restless, Un-ruh-e unrest, Ruhe-pause a break, Ruhe-störung a disturbance, ruh-en to rest (Ruhe in Frieden renders requiescat in pace), aus-ruh-en to rest up, ruhe-los restless, ge-ruh-sam leisurely; Rast is a different word (rest)."],
  biet: ["bieten(提供)来自原始日耳曼语 *beudaną(命令、宣告、提供),与英语 bid(古义命令、宣告——forbid、bid 出价)、荷兰语 bieden、哥特语 anabiudan 同根;印欧语 *bʰeudʰ-(察觉、使知道)——与希腊语 pynthanesthai(打听)、梵语 bodhati(觉悟——Buddha 是\"觉悟者\")、俄语 budit'(唤醒)同源:提供是\"让人知道有\"。An-ge-bot 报价、供应(\"提出的\"),an-biet-en 提供,Ge-bot 诫命(十诫 die Zehn Gebote)、出价,ver-biet-en 禁止(forbid 同构),Ver-bot 禁令,ge-biet-en 命令、要求,Ge-biet 地区(\"管辖范围\"),Ge-biet-er 主人,Auf-ge-bot 动员、结婚公告,Bot-e 信使(\"被派去宣告的\"),Bot-schaft 消息、大使馆(\"送信处\"),Bot-schaft-er 大使,ent-biet-en 致意,er-biet-en 主动提出,unter-biet-en 压价,über-biet-en 超过,Vor-ge-bot?否,Bei-be-halten 无关,Ge-bots-schild 指示牌。",
    "bieten (to offer) comes from Proto-Germanic *beudaną (to command, announce, offer), kin to English bid (once to command, proclaim — forbid, and to bid at auction), Dutch bieden, Gothic anabiudan; PIE *bʰeudʰ- (to be aware, make aware) — with Greek pynthanesthai (to inquire), Sanskrit bodhati (awakens — Buddha is the Awakened), Russian budit' (to wake): to offer is to make known that something is there. An-ge-bot an offer, supply, an-biet-en to offer, Ge-bot a commandment (die Zehn Gebote), a bid, ver-biet-en to forbid (the same build), Ver-bot a ban, ge-biet-en to command, to require, Ge-biet a region (the range of command), Ge-biet-er a master, Auf-ge-bot mobilisation, banns, Bot-e a messenger (one sent to proclaim), Bot-schaft a message, an embassy (where messages are sent), Bot-schaft-er an ambassador, ent-biet-en to send greetings, er-biet-en to volunteer, unter-biet-en to undercut, über-biet-en to outdo, Ge-bots-schild a mandatory sign."],
  acht: ["Acht(注意)来自原始日耳曼语 *ahtō(考虑、留心),与古英语 eaht(评估)、荷兰语 acht、哥特语 aha(理智)同根;印欧语 *ok-(思量)存疑。acht-en 注意、尊重,Acht-ung 尊敬、\"注意!\",be-acht-en 注意、遵守,Be-acht-ung 注意,be-acht-lich 可观的,ver-acht-en 鄙视(\"不看重\"),Ver-acht-ung 蔑视,ver-ächt-lich 轻蔑的,be-ob-acht-en 观察(ob- 是 auf,\"留心看\"——observare 的直译),Be-ob-acht-ung 观察,Acht-sam-keit 正念(佛教 mindfulness 的德译),un-acht-sam 疏忽,acht-los 漫不经心,miss-acht-en 无视,Miss-acht-ung 藐视,Nicht-acht-ung 不尊重,acht-geben 留神,Acht(八)是另一个词(*ahtōu,与拉丁语 octo 同根),Ächt-ung 放逐(又一个词,古高地德语 āhta 追捕——outlaw)。",
    "Acht (heed) comes from Proto-Germanic *ahtō (consideration, attention), kin to Old English eaht (estimation), Dutch acht, Gothic aha (understanding); PIE *ok- (to consider) is possible. acht-en to heed, to respect, Acht-ung respect, and \"attention!\", be-acht-en to note, to observe (rules), Be-acht-ung notice, be-acht-lich considerable, ver-acht-en to despise (to hold of no account), Ver-acht-ung contempt, ver-ächt-lich contemptuous, be-ob-acht-en to observe (ob- is auf — to keep watch; a calque of observare), Be-ob-acht-ung an observation, Acht-sam-keit mindfulness (the German for the Buddhist term), un-acht-sam careless, acht-los heedless, miss-acht-en to disregard, Miss-acht-ung disdain, acht-geben to take care; acht (eight) is a different word (*ahtōu, kin to Latin octo), and Ächt-ung outlawry yet another (Old High German āhta, pursuit)."],
  mess: ["messen(量)来自原始日耳曼语 *metaną,印欧语 *med-(量、思量)——与拉丁语 modus、mederi、meditari、希腊语 medomai、英语 mete(量)、meal(一餐——\"定量\";Mahl)同根。Maß 尺度、节制(\"量出的\";Maß-krug 一升啤酒杯),Maß-nahme 措施(\"取尺度\"),Maß-stab 标准、比例尺(\"量尺\"),mäß-ig 适度的、平庸的,-mäß-ig 后缀\"按…的\"(regel-mäßig 规律的、zeit-mäßig),Mäß-ig-ung 节制,über-mäß-ig 过度,an-ge-mess-en 恰当(\"量好的\"),Ab-mess-ung 尺寸,Aus-maß 程度,Ver-mess-ung 测量,ver-mess-en 测量、也指狂妄(\"量错了\"),be-mess-en 计量,Ge-mäß 按照,zweck-mäßig 合目的,Mess-er 刀是另一个词(mezzi-sahs 食刀),Mess-e 弥撒、博览会(拉丁语 missa——弥撒后的集市!),Ebenmaß 匀称,Mahl 饭食(同根),Mahlzeit 用餐。",
    "messen (to measure) comes from Proto-Germanic *metaną, PIE *med- (to measure, consider) — kin to Latin modus, mederi, meditari, Greek medomai, English mete, meal (a portion measured out; German Mahl). Maß measure, moderation (a Maß-krug is a litre beer mug), Maß-nahme a measure (taking a measure), Maß-stab a standard, a scale (measuring-stick), mäß-ig moderate, mediocre, the suffix -mäß-ig \"according to\" (regel-mäßig regular, zeit-mäßig), Mäß-ig-ung restraint, über-mäß-ig excessive, an-ge-mess-en appropriate (well measured), Ab-mess-ung a dimension, Aus-maß extent, Ver-mess-ung surveying, ver-mess-en to survey, and presumptuous (mis-measured), be-mess-en to allot, ge-mäß in accordance with, zweck-mäßig fit for purpose; Mess-er (knife) is another word (mezzi-sahs, a food-knife), and Mess-e is the Mass and a trade fair (Latin missa — the market held after Mass!), Ebenmaß symmetry, Mahl a meal, Mahlzeit."],
  schaff: ["schaffen 是两个动词:强变化 schaffen/schuf(创造——Gott schuf)来自原始日耳曼语 *skapjaną(削、造),与英语 shape(古英语 scieppan 造)、荷兰语 scheppen、-schaft/-ship 后缀同根;弱变化 schaffen/schaffte(完成、劳作)是它的南德变体。印欧语 *skep-(削、切)——与 Schaft(杆,削出的)、拉丁语 无(capere 无关)同源。Schöpf-er 造物主,Schöpf-ung 创世,schöpf-erisch 有创造力,Ge-schöpf 受造物,er-schaff-en 创造,ab-schaff-en 废除(\"造掉\"),Ab-schaff-ung 废除,an-schaff-en 购置,An-schaff-ung 购置,be-schaff-en 采购、具有…性质,Be-schaff-en-heit 性质(\"造成的样子\"),ver-schaff-en 弄到,Schaff-ner 售票员(\"管事的\"),schaff-en 干活(南德:Ich schaff’s 我能行),-schaft 后缀(Freund-schaft、Wissen-schaft、Gesell-schaft——\"造出的状态\";英语 -ship),Land-schaft 风景(荷兰画派把它带进英语 landscape),Wirt-schaft 经济(\"主人的经营\")。",
    "schaffen is two verbs: the strong schaffen/schuf (to create — Gott schuf) from Proto-Germanic *skapjaną (to carve, form), kin to English shape (Old English scieppan, to create), Dutch scheppen and the suffix -schaft/-ship; the weak schaffen/schaffte (to manage, to toil) is its southern variant. PIE *skep- (to cut, hew) — with Schaft (a shaft, hewn). Schöpf-er the Creator, Schöpf-ung creation, schöpf-erisch creative, Ge-schöpf a creature, er-schaff-en to create, ab-schaff-en to abolish (to shape away), Ab-schaff-ung abolition, an-schaff-en to acquire, An-schaff-ung a purchase, be-schaff-en to procure, and constituted, Be-schaff-en-heit quality (the way it is made), ver-schaff-en to obtain, Schaff-ner a conductor (the one who manages), schaff-en to work (southern: Ich schaff's, I can do it), the suffix -schaft (Freund-schaft, Wissen-schaft, Gesell-schaft — the shaped state; English -ship), Land-schaft landscape (the Dutch painters carried it into English), Wirt-schaft the economy (the host's housekeeping)."],
  lös: ["lösen(松开、解决)来自原始日耳曼语 *lausijaną(使松),是 los(松的)的使役式;印欧语 *leu-(松开)——与拉丁语 solvere(se-luere)、luere、希腊语 lyein(lysis)、英语 loose、lose、-less(无)同根。Lös-ung 解决、溶液(solutio 的两义并存!),los 松的、走吧(Los! 开始!),Los 签、命运(\"分出的份\"——lot),-los 后缀\"无\"(arbeits-los 失业、hoffnungs-los 绝望;英语 -less),los-lassen 放开,ab-lös-en 取代、脱落,Ab-lös-ung 换班、替代,auf-lös-en 溶解、解散,Auf-lös-ung 解散、分辨率,aus-lös-en 引发、赎出,Aus-lös-er 快门、诱因,ein-lös-en 兑现,er-lös-en 拯救(\"解开\"——路德译 redimere),Er-lös-er 救主,Er-lös 收益(\"解出的钱\"),Ver-lust 损失(ver-lieren——\"松掉\";英语 lose),lös-bar 可解的,un-lös-lich 不溶的,lös-lich 可溶,Los-ung 口令,er-lös-t 解脱的,Ver-lös-ung?否。",
    "lösen (to loosen, to solve) comes from Proto-Germanic *lausijaną (to make loose), the causative of los (loose); PIE *leu- (to loosen) — kin to Latin solvere (se-luere), luere, Greek lyein (lysis), English loose, lose, -less. Lös-ung a solution (both senses of solutio!), los loose, and go! (Los! start!), Los a lot, fate (the share dealt out), the suffix -los without (arbeits-los unemployed, hoffnungs-los hopeless; English -less), los-lassen to let go, ab-lös-en to replace, to come off, Ab-lös-ung a relief, a replacement, auf-lös-en to dissolve, to disband, Auf-lös-ung dissolution, resolution (of a screen), aus-lös-en to trigger, to redeem, Aus-lös-er a shutter release, a trigger, ein-lös-en to cash in, er-lös-en to redeem (to loose — Luther's word for redimere), Er-lös-er the Redeemer, Er-lös proceeds (money loosed), Ver-lust loss (ver-lieren, to lose hold; English lose), lös-bar solvable, un-lös-lich insoluble, lös-lich soluble, Los-ung a password."],
  geh: ["gehen(走)来自原始日耳曼语 *gāną,印欧语 *ǵʰeh₁-(走、离开)——与希腊语 kikhanō(到达)、梵语 jahāti(离开)、英语 go 同根;过去式 ging 来自另一个词根 *ganganą(Gang 走廊、步态——英语 gang 本是\"一行人\")。Gang 走、走廊、挡位、菜(一道),Aus-gang 出口、结果,Ein-gang 入口,Über-gang 过渡,Unter-gang 沉没、灭亡(\"走到底下\"——斯宾格勒的 Untergang des Abendlandes),Um-gang 交往,Vor-gang 过程、事件,Zu-gang 入口、访问权,Rück-gang 下降,Auf-gang 楼梯、日出,Durch-gang 通道,Ab-gang 离去,Nieder-gang 衰落,Ge-gen-wart 无关,gang-bar 可行的,gäng-ig 通行的,Fuß-gäng-er 行人,Vor-gäng-er 前任,Nach-gäng-er?否,ver-gang-en 过去的,Ver-gang-en-heit 过去(\"走过去了\"),Ver-gäng-lich-keit 无常,Gang-art 步态,gang und gäbe 司空见惯。",
    "gehen (to go) comes from Proto-Germanic *gāną, PIE *ǵʰeh₁- (to go, leave) — kin to Greek kikhanō (to reach), Sanskrit jahāti (leaves), English go; the past tense ging comes from a second root *ganganą (Gang a corridor, a gait — English gang was once a going, a band). Gang a walk, a corridor, a gear, a course (of a meal), Aus-gang an exit, an outcome, Ein-gang an entrance, Über-gang a transition, Unter-gang a sinking, a downfall (going under — Spengler's Untergang des Abendlandes), Um-gang dealings, Vor-gang a process, an incident, Zu-gang access, Rück-gang a decline, Auf-gang a staircase, a sunrise, Durch-gang a passage, Ab-gang a departure, Nieder-gang decline, gang-bar viable, gäng-ig current, Fuß-gäng-er a pedestrian, Vor-gäng-er a predecessor, ver-gang-en past, Ver-gang-en-heit the past (gone by), Ver-gäng-lich-keit transience, Gang-art a gait, gang und gäbe customary."],
  find: ["finden(找到)来自原始日耳曼语 *finþaną,印欧语 *pent-(走、踩路)——与拉丁语 pons(桥,属格 pontis——\"路\")、希腊语 pontos(海——\"通路\")、patos(路)、梵语 pánthāḥ(路)、英语 find、俄语 put'(路)同根:找到就是\"踩出路来\"。Fund 发现物(Fund-büro 失物招领处,Fund-ort 出土地),Er-find-ung 发明(\"找出来\"),er-find-en 发明,Er-find-er 发明家,Be-fund 检查结果、诊断,be-find-en 觉得、位于,Be-find-en 健康状况,emp-find-en 感觉(\"感受到\"——ent-finden),Emp-find-ung 感觉,emp-find-lich 敏感、易怒,emp-find-sam 多愁善感(18 世纪 Empfindsamkeit 感伤主义),ab-find-en 补偿、认命,Ab-find-ung 遣散费,statt-find-en 举行(\"找到地方\"),vor-find-en 发现,heraus-find-en 查明,find-ig 机灵的,Find-ling 弃婴、漂砾,Fund-ament 是拉丁语(fundus),Pfad 小路(同根,path)。",
    "finden (to find) comes from Proto-Germanic *finþaną, PIE *pent- (to go, tread a path) — kin to Latin pons (a bridge, genitive pontis — a way), Greek pontos (the sea — a passage), patos (a path), Sanskrit pánthāḥ (a road), English find, Russian put' (a way): to find is to tread out a path. Fund a find (Fund-büro lost property, Fund-ort a find-spot), Er-find-ung an invention (found out), er-find-en to invent, Er-find-er an inventor, Be-fund findings, a diagnosis, be-find-en to feel, to be located, Be-find-en state of health, emp-find-en to feel (ent-finden), Emp-find-ung a sensation, emp-find-lich sensitive, touchy, emp-find-sam sentimental (the 18th-century Empfindsamkeit), ab-find-en to compensate, to resign oneself, Ab-find-ung severance pay, statt-find-en to take place (to find a place), vor-find-en to come upon, heraus-find-en to find out, find-ig resourceful, Find-ling a foundling, an erratic boulder, Pfad a path (same root)."],
  lauf: ["laufen(跑、走)来自原始日耳曼语 *hlaupaną(跳),与英语 leap、lope(经古挪威语)、荷兰语 lopen、哥特语 hlaupan 同根;词源可能是日耳曼语独有的(与立陶宛语 klupti 绊倒?存疑)。日耳曼语的\"跳\"在德语里变成\"跑\",在荷兰语里变成\"走\"。Lauf 跑、进程、枪管,Läuf-er 跑者、长毯、象(国际象棋),Ab-lauf 流程、到期(\"跑完\"),Ver-lauf 过程,Vor-lauf 预赛、提前量,Um-lauf 流通(\"绕着跑\"——circulatio),Zu-lauf 客流,Auf-lauf 人群、烤菜,Ein-lauf 到达,Aus-lauf 跑动空间、停产,An-lauf 助跑、开始,Rück-lauf 回流,Wett-lauf 赛跑,Zeit-lauf?否,lauf-end 当前的、持续的,ver-lauf-en 进行、迷路,Ge-läuf-ig 熟悉的(\"常跑的\"),vor-läuf-ig 暂时的,läuf-ig 发情的,ent-lauf-en 逃跑,über-lauf-en 溢出、叛逃,Über-läuf-er 叛逃者,Lauf-bahn 生涯(\"跑道\"——curriculum 的直译),Lauf-werk 驱动器。",
    "laufen (to run, to walk) comes from Proto-Germanic *hlaupaną (to leap), kin to English leap and lope (via Old Norse), Dutch lopen, Gothic hlaupan; the root is probably purely Germanic. The Germanic leap became running in German and walking in Dutch. Lauf a run, a course, a gun-barrel, Läuf-er a runner, a rug, a bishop (in chess), Ab-lauf a sequence, expiry (run out), Ver-lauf a course, Vor-lauf a heat, a lead, Um-lauf circulation (running round — circulatio), Zu-lauf an influx, Auf-lauf a crowd, a bake, Ein-lauf arrival, Aus-lauf room to run, discontinuation, An-lauf a run-up, a start, Rück-lauf a return flow, Wett-lauf a race, lauf-end current, ongoing, ver-lauf-en to proceed, to lose one's way, ge-läuf-ig familiar (often run), vor-läuf-ig provisional, ent-lauf-en to run away, über-lauf-en to overflow, to defect, Über-läuf-er a defector, Lauf-bahn a career (a running track — a calque of curriculum), Lauf-werk a drive."],
  lieb: ["lieb(亲爱的)来自原始日耳曼语 *leubaz,印欧语 *leubʰ-(爱、渴望)——与拉丁语 libet(合意)、libido、俄语 ljubit'(爱)、英语 love、lief(乐意)、believe、leave(许可——\"喜欢\"→\"允许\")同根;glauben、erlauben、Urlaub、loben 是它的德语亲戚。Lieb-e 爱,lieb-en 爱,Lieb-haber 爱好者、情人(\"有爱的人\"),Lieb-ling 宠儿(\"小爱\"——darling 同构),be-lieb-t 受欢迎,Be-lieb-t-heit 人气,be-lieb-ig 任意的(\"随喜\"),ver-lieb-en 爱上,ver-lieb-t 恋爱中,Lieb-es-brief 情书,lieb-lich 可爱的、甜的(酒),Lieb-reiz 魅力,lieb-los 冷淡,Vor-lieb-e 偏爱,lieb-er 宁可(比较级——\"更喜欢\"),am lieb-sten 最喜欢,Lieb-ste 最爱的人,Lob 赞扬,lob-en 赞扬,ge-lob-en 誓言,Ur-laub 假期,Er-laub-nis 许可,Glaub-e 信仰,Lieb-frauen-kirche 圣母教堂。",
    "lieb (dear) comes from Proto-Germanic *leubaz, PIE *leubʰ- (to love, desire) — kin to Latin libet (it pleases), libido, Russian ljubit' (to love), English love, lief (gladly), believe, leave (permission — liking → allowing); glauben, erlauben, Urlaub and loben are its German relatives. Lieb-e love, lieb-en to love, Lieb-haber a lover, an enthusiast (one who has love), Lieb-ling a darling (little love — the same build), be-lieb-t popular, Be-lieb-t-heit popularity, be-lieb-ig any (at one's pleasure), ver-lieb-en to fall in love, ver-lieb-t in love, Lieb-es-brief a love letter, lieb-lich lovely, sweet (of wine), Lieb-reiz charm, lieb-los loveless, Vor-lieb-e a preference, lieb-er rather (the comparative — more gladly), am lieb-sten most of all, Lieb-ste one's beloved, Lob praise, lob-en to praise, ge-lob-en to vow, Ur-laub leave, Er-laub-nis permission, Glaub-e faith, Lieb-frauen-kirche the Church of Our Lady."],
  fürcht: ["Furcht(恐惧)来自原始日耳曼语 *furhtō,与英语 fright(古英语 fyrhto)、荷兰语 vrucht?否——荷兰语 vrees 是另一词,哥特语 faurhtei 同根;印欧语 *perk-(害怕)存疑,同根的可能有托卡里安语 pärsk-(害怕)。fürcht-en 害怕,sich fürcht-en 害怕,fürcht-er-lich 可怕的,furcht-bar 可怕的(也作强调:furchtbar nett 非常好),furcht-los 无畏,furcht-sam 胆小,be-fürcht-en 担心,Be-fürcht-ung 担忧,Ehr-furcht 敬畏(\"荣誉的恐惧\"——对神与长者),ehr-fürcht-ig 敬畏的,Gottes-furcht 敬畏神,furcht-erregend 骇人,Furcht-samkeit 胆怯,fright 是它的英语兄弟(frighten、frightful)。",
    "Furcht (fear) comes from Proto-Germanic *furhtō, kin to English fright (Old English fyrhto) and Gothic faurhtei (Dutch vrees is a different word); PIE *perk- (to fear) is possible, with Tocharian pärsk- (to be afraid) as a cousin. fürcht-en to fear, sich fürcht-en to be afraid, fürcht-er-lich dreadful, furcht-bar terrible (and as an intensifier: furchtbar nett, terribly nice), furcht-los fearless, furcht-sam timid, be-fürcht-en to fear that, Be-fürcht-ung an apprehension, Ehr-furcht awe (the fear of honour — before gods and elders), ehr-fürcht-ig reverent, Gottes-furcht the fear of God, furcht-erregend frightening, Furcht-samkeit timidity; fright is its English brother (frighten, frightful)."],
  trink: ["trinken(喝)来自原始日耳曼语 *drinkaną,与英语 drink、荷兰语 drinken、哥特语 drigkan 同根;词源可能是日耳曼语独有(与 *dragan 拉——\"吸\"有关?存疑)。Trank 饮料、药水(Zauber-trank 魔药,Trunk 饮),Trunk-en-heit 醉(\"喝了的状态\"——drunk),be-trunk-en 醉的,Be-trunk-ener 醉汉,er-trink-en 溺死(\"喝完了\"——drown 的德语兄弟是 ertrinken,英语 drench 是使役式\"使喝\"),er-tränk-en 淹死(使役),trink-bar 可饮用的,Trink-wasser 饮用水,Trink-geld 小费(\"喝酒钱\"——pourboire 的直译),Trink-spruch 祝酒词,an-trink-en 喝到微醺,aus-trink-en 喝光,Trink-halm 吸管,Ge-tränk 饮料,Um-trunk 聚饮,Trunk-sucht 酗酒,tränk-en 饮牲口、浸透,Tränk-e 饮水槽,Trink-er 酒徒。",
    "trinken (to drink) comes from Proto-Germanic *drinkaną, kin to English drink, Dutch drinken, Gothic drigkan; the root is probably purely Germanic. Trank a drink, a potion (Zauber-trank a magic potion; Trunk a draught), Trunk-en-heit drunkenness, be-trunk-en drunk, Be-trunk-ener a drunk, er-trink-en to drown (drink one's fill — English drench is the causative, to make drink), er-tränk-en to drown someone, trink-bar drinkable, Trink-wasser drinking water, Trink-geld a tip (drink-money — a calque of pourboire), Trink-spruch a toast, an-trink-en to get tipsy, aus-trink-en to drink up, Trink-halm a straw, Ge-tränk a beverage, Um-trunk a round of drinks, Trunk-sucht alcoholism, tränk-en to water animals, to soak, Tränk-e a trough, Trink-er a drinker."],
  buch: ["Buch(书)来自原始日耳曼语 *bōks(书写板、字母)——与 Buche(山毛榉)同根:日耳曼人在山毛榉木板上刻卢恩符文,所以\"书\"就是\"榉木片\";英语 book/beech、荷兰语 boek/beuk、俄语 bukva(字母,借自哥特语)都是这一对。Buch-stabe 字母(\"书棍\"——刻符文的小木棍),buch-stab-ieren 拼写,buch-stäb-lich 逐字的,Büch-er-ei 图书馆,Buch-hand-lung 书店,Buch-halt-ung 簿记(\"记账\"),Buch-halt-er 会计,buch-en 预订、入账,Buch-ung 预订,ver-buch-en 记账、取得,Buch-druck 印刷术,Buch-binder 装订工,Buch-macher 赌注登记人(英语 bookmaker 的借译),Buch-weizen 荞麦(\"榉果状的麦\"),Hand-buch 手册,Wörter-buch 词典,Lehr-buch 教科书,Tage-buch 日记,Kur-buch 时刻表,Buch-messe 书展(法兰克福,1454 年起)。",
    "Buch (a book) comes from Proto-Germanic *bōks (a writing-tablet, a letter) — the same root as Buche (the beech): Germanic peoples cut runes into beech-wood staves, so a book is a beech-slab; English book/beech, Dutch boek/beuk, Russian bukva (a letter, borrowed from Gothic) are the same pair. Buch-stabe a letter of the alphabet (a book-stave — the rune-stick), buch-stab-ieren to spell, buch-stäb-lich literal, Büch-er-ei a library, Buch-hand-lung a bookshop, Buch-halt-ung bookkeeping, Buch-halt-er an accountant, buch-en to book, to enter, Buch-ung a booking, ver-buch-en to record, to chalk up, Buch-druck printing, Buch-binder a bookbinder, Buch-macher a bookmaker (calqued from English), Buch-weizen buckwheat (beech-nut wheat), Hand-buch a handbook, Wörter-buch a dictionary, Lehr-buch a textbook, Tage-buch a diary, Buch-messe a book fair (Frankfurt, since 1454)."],
  welt: ["Welt(世界)是古高地德语 weralt 的缩合:wer(人——拉丁语 vir、英语 werewolf 的 were)+ alt(年岁、时代):\"人的时代\"——一个人活着的时间,然后是人活着的空间;英语 world(wer-old)、荷兰语 wereld 同构。它是日耳曼人对拉丁语 saeculum(世代、尘世)的翻译式创造。Welt-all 宇宙(\"世界之全\"),Welt-anschauung 世界观(康德以后的哲学词,进了英语),Welt-krieg 世界大战(1914),Welt-meister 世界冠军,welt-weit 全球的,Welt-raum 太空,Welt-reich 帝国,Um-welt 环境(\"周围的世界\"——1909 年生物学家于克斯屈尔造),Mit-welt 同时代人,Nach-welt 后世,Vor-welt 史前,Welt-schmerz 世界之痛(让·保罗 1827),Welt-stadt 大都市,Unter-welt 冥界、黑社会,Welt-wunder 世界奇迹,welt-lich 世俗的(saecularis 的直译),Welt-bürger 世界公民,Welt-literatur 世界文学(歌德 1827)。",
    "Welt (the world) is Old High German weralt contracted: wer (man — Latin vir, the were of werewolf) + alt (age, era): the age of man — the span of a life, then the space one lives in; English world (wer-old) and Dutch wereld are built the same way, a Germanic rendering of Latin saeculum (an age, the temporal world). Welt-all the universe (the world's all), Welt-anschauung a world-view (post-Kantian, borrowed into English), Welt-krieg world war (1914), Welt-meister a world champion, welt-weit worldwide, Welt-raum outer space, Welt-reich an empire, Um-welt the environment (the surrounding world — coined by the biologist Uexküll in 1909), Mit-welt one's contemporaries, Nach-welt posterity, Vor-welt prehistory, Welt-schmerz world-weariness (Jean Paul 1827), Welt-stadt a metropolis, Unter-welt the underworld, Welt-wunder a wonder of the world, welt-lich worldly (a calque of saecularis), Welt-bürger a citizen of the world, Welt-literatur world literature (Goethe 1827)."],
  bind: ["binden(绑)来自原始日耳曼语 *bindaną,印欧语 *bʰendʰ-(绑)——与梵语 badhnāti(绑)、希腊语 peisma(缆绳)、拉丁语 offendix(帽带)、英语 bind、band、bond、bundle 同根。Band 带子、卷(书)、乐队(英语 band 经法语——同一个根绕了一圈),Bund 联盟、联邦(\"绑在一起\"——Bundes-republik 联邦共和国、Bundes-tag),Bünd-nis 同盟,ver-bind-en 连接、包扎,Ver-bind-ung 联系、化合物,ver-bind-lich 有约束力的、友好的,Ver-band 绷带、协会,Ver-bünd-ete 盟友,ent-bind-en 分娩(\"解开\")、免除,Ent-bind-ung 分娩,Ein-band 封面,Bind-ung 约束、纽带,An-bind-ung 连接,Bind-e-strich 连字符,Bind-e-wort 连词,Bündel 捆,bünd-ig 简明的(\"扎紧的\"),Bindfaden 细绳,Um-bind-en 系上,Bündnis-fall 同盟情况,Ge-bunden 被绑的、精装的,Band-wurm 绦虫。",
    "binden (to bind) comes from Proto-Germanic *bindaną, PIE *bʰendʰ- (to bind) — kin to Sanskrit badhnāti (binds), Greek peisma (a cable), Latin offendix (a hat-string), English bind, band, bond, bundle. Band a ribbon, a volume, a band (English band came back through French — the same root round the loop), Bund an alliance, a federation (bound together — Bundes-republik, Bundes-tag), Bünd-nis an alliance, ver-bind-en to connect, to bandage, Ver-bind-ung a connection, a compound, ver-bind-lich binding, obliging, Ver-band a bandage, an association, Ver-bünd-ete allies, ent-bind-en to give birth (to unbind), to release, Ent-bind-ung childbirth, Ein-band a book cover, Bind-ung a bond, a tie, An-bind-ung a link, Bind-e-strich a hyphen, Bind-e-wort a conjunction, Bündel a bundle, bünd-ig concise (tied tight), Bindfaden string, um-bind-en to tie on, ge-bunden bound, hardback, Band-wurm a tapeworm."],
  sag: ["sagen(说)来自原始日耳曼语 *sagjaną,印欧语 *sekʷ-(说、看、跟随)——与拉丁语 inquam(我说)、insece(讲述)、希腊语 ennepe(讲述——《奥德赛》第一行 andra moi ennepe)、英语 say、saw(格言)、saga(经古挪威语)、立陶宛语 sakyti 同根:同一个 *sekʷ- 在拉丁语里是 sequi(跟随)——说是让话跟着事走。Sag-e 传说(saga 的德语形式),sag-en-haft 传奇的,Aus-sag-e 陈述、证词,ab-sag-en 取消,Ab-sag-e 拒绝,an-sag-en 宣布,An-sag-e 广播通知,zu-sag-en 答应,Zu-sag-e 承诺,ver-sag-en 失败、拒绝(\"说错\"→失灵),Ver-sag-en 失灵,Ver-sag-er 失败者,be-sag-en 意味着,ent-sag-en 放弃,Ent-sag-ung 克己,vor-her-sag-en 预言,Vor-her-sag-e 预报,Wahr-sag-er 占卜者,un-säg-lich 说不出的,Sag-ung?否,weis-sag-en 预言,Bei-sag-e?否,Sprich-wort 无关,Sag-bare 可说的。",
    "sagen (to say) comes from Proto-Germanic *sagjaną, PIE *sekʷ- (to say, see, follow) — kin to Latin inquam (I say), insece (tell!), Greek ennepe (tell — the first line of the Odyssey, andra moi ennepe), English say, saw (a saying), saga (via Old Norse), Lithuanian sakyti: the same *sekʷ- is Latin sequi (to follow) — to say is to make words follow the facts. Sag-e a legend (saga in German dress), sag-en-haft legendary, Aus-sag-e a statement, testimony, ab-sag-en to cancel, Ab-sag-e a refusal, an-sag-en to announce, An-sag-e an announcement, zu-sag-en to accept, Zu-sag-e a commitment, ver-sag-en to fail, to deny (to misspeak → to malfunction), Ver-sag-en failure, Ver-sag-er a loser, be-sag-en to mean, ent-sag-en to renounce, Ent-sag-ung renunciation, vor-her-sag-en to predict, Vor-her-sag-e a forecast, Wahr-sag-er a fortune-teller, un-säg-lich unspeakable, weis-sag-en to prophesy."],
  führ: ["führen(引导)是 fahren(行驶)的使役式——\"使行走\":原始日耳曼语 *fōrijaną;与英语 ferry(渡)、fare、荷兰语 voeren 同根,印欧语 *per-。Führ-er 领袖、司机、指南(1933 年后成为希特勒的头衔,今天德语只在复合词里用:Reise-führer 导游,Ge-schäfts-führer 总经理,Führer-schein 驾照),Führ-ung 领导、导览,An-führ-er 首领,an-führ-en 率领、引用、愚弄,Auf-führ-ung 演出(\"演上来\"),auf-führ-en 上演、举止,Aus-führ-ung 执行、款式,aus-führ-en 出口、执行、阐述,Ein-führ-ung 引入、导论,ein-führ-en 进口、引进,durch-führ-en 实施,Durch-führ-ung 实施,ver-führ-en 引诱(\"引错\"),Ver-führ-ung 诱惑,Ver-führ-er 诱惑者,fort-führ-en 继续,zurück-führ-en 归因,Rück-führ-ung 遣返,über-führ-en 转移、定罪,Ge-führ-te?否,Für-sorge 无关,Führ-ungs-kraft 高管,Buch-führ-ung 簿记。",
    "führen (to lead) is the causative of fahren (to go) — to make go: Proto-Germanic *fōrijaną; kin to English ferry, fare, Dutch voeren, PIE *per-. Führ-er a leader, a driver, a guide (Hitler's title after 1933, so today German uses it only in compounds: Reise-führer a tour guide, Ge-schäfts-führer a managing director, Führer-schein a driving licence), Führ-ung leadership, a guided tour, An-führ-er a ringleader, an-führ-en to lead, to cite, to fool, Auf-führ-ung a performance (led up onto the stage), auf-führ-en to perform, to behave, Aus-führ-ung execution, a version, aus-führ-en to export, to carry out, to explain, Ein-führ-ung an introduction, ein-führ-en to import, to introduce, durch-führ-en to implement, Durch-führ-ung implementation, ver-führ-en to seduce (to lead astray), Ver-führ-ung temptation, Ver-führ-er a seducer, fort-führ-en to continue, zurück-führ-en to trace back, Rück-führ-ung repatriation, über-führ-en to transfer, to convict, Führ-ungs-kraft an executive, Buch-führ-ung bookkeeping."],
  schein: ["scheinen(照耀、显得)来自原始日耳曼语 *skīnaną,印欧语 *skeh₂i-(闪、影)——与希腊语 skia(影子——skia 与光同根!)、梵语 chāyā(影)、英语 shine、shimmer、俄语 sijat'(照耀)同根:照与影是一回事的两面。Schein 光、外表、票据(Geld-schein 钞票、Führer-schein 驾照、Gut-schein 优惠券——\"证明的纸\"),schein-bar 表面上的,an-schein-end 显然,An-schein 表象,er-schein-en 出现、出版,Er-schein-ung 现象、幻影(Kant 的 Erscheinung 译 phainomenon),Schein-werfer 探照灯、车灯,Schein-heil-ig 伪善(\"看起来神圣\"),Schein-tod 假死,Schein-ehe 假婚,be-schein-ig-en 证明,Be-schein-ig-ung 证明书,Sonnen-schein 阳光,Mond-schein 月光,Heilig-en-schein 光环,Wider-schein 反光,Voll-schein?否,un-schein-bar 不起眼,Schein-welt 虚幻世界,Trug-schein?否。",
    "scheinen (to shine, to seem) comes from Proto-Germanic *skīnaną, PIE *skeh₂i- (to gleam, shadow) — kin to Greek skia (a shadow — shadow and light share a root!), Sanskrit chāyā (shade), English shine, shimmer, Russian sijat' (to shine). Schein light, appearance, a certificate (Geld-schein a banknote, Führer-schein a driving licence, Gut-schein a voucher — the paper that shows), schein-bar seeming, an-schein-end apparently, An-schein appearance, er-schein-en to appear, to be published, Er-schein-ung a phenomenon, an apparition (Kant's Erscheinung for phainomenon), Schein-werfer a floodlight, a headlight, schein-heil-ig hypocritical (seemingly holy), Schein-tod apparent death, Schein-ehe a sham marriage, be-schein-ig-en to certify, Be-schein-ig-ung a certificate, Sonnen-schein sunshine, Mond-schein moonlight, Heilig-en-schein a halo, Wider-schein a reflection, un-schein-bar inconspicuous, Schein-welt a world of illusion."],
  werd: ["werden(变成)来自原始日耳曼语 *werþaną(转、变),印欧语 *wert-(转)——与拉丁语 vertere、梵语 vartate(转)、俄语 vertet'、英语 worth(古义\"变成\"——woe worth the day)、-ward(toward 朝向)同根:变成就是\"转成\"。它是德语的助动词:将来时(ich werde gehen)、被动(wird gebaut),都是\"转向\"的隐喻。Wert 价值(\"转来的东西\"——英语 worth)也是这个根,wert-voll 珍贵,Wert-ung 评价,be-wert-en 评估,Be-wert-ung 评估,ent-wert-en 贬值,auf-wert-en 升值,Wert-schätz-ung 重视,wert-los 无价值,-wert 后缀\"值得\"(sehens-wert 值得看、lebens-wert),Wert-papier 有价证券,Mehr-wert 附加值(马克思的剩余价值 Mehrwert),Ge-gen-wart 现在(\"转向的对面\"——praesens),Werd-e-gang 生涯,Wurde?否,Um-wert-ung 价值重估(尼采),Würde 尊严(\"值得的状态\"),würd-ig 值得的,Würd-en-träger 显贵。",
    "werden (to become) comes from Proto-Germanic *werþaną (to turn, become), PIE *wert- (to turn) — kin to Latin vertere, Sanskrit vartate (turns), Russian vertet', English worth (once \"to become\" — woe worth the day), -ward (toward): to become is to turn into. It is German's auxiliary of the future (ich werde gehen) and the passive (wird gebaut) — both a turning. Wert (value — English worth) is the same root, wert-voll valuable, Wert-ung a rating, be-wert-en to evaluate, Be-wert-ung an assessment, ent-wert-en to devalue, auf-wert-en to upgrade, Wert-schätz-ung esteem, wert-los worthless, the suffix -wert worth (sehens-wert worth seeing, lebens-wert), Wert-papier a security, Mehr-wert added value (Marx's surplus value, Mehrwert), Ge-gen-wart the present (what has turned to face us — praesens), Werd-e-gang a career path, Um-wert-ung a revaluation of values (Nietzsche), Würde dignity (the state of being worth), würd-ig worthy, Würd-en-träger a dignitary."],
  nenn: ["nennen(命名)来自原始日耳曼语 *namnijaną——\"给名字\",是 Name(名)的动词;Name 来自印欧语 *h₁nómn̥,与拉丁语 nomen、希腊语 onoma、英语 name、梵语 nāman 同根。Nam-e 名,nam-ent-lich 尤其(\"按名说\"),nam-haft 著名,Nam-ens-tag 命名日,nam-en-los 无名,Bei-nam-e 绰号,Vor-nam-e 名,Nach-nam-e 姓,Deck-nam-e 化名,Spitz-nam-e 外号,be-nenn-en 命名,Be-nenn-ung 名称,er-nenn-en 任命(\"叫出名字\"),Er-nenn-ung 任命,Nenn-er 分母(\"命名者\"——denominator 的直译),Nenn-wert 面值(nominal 的直译),Nenn-ung 提名,so-ge-nannt 所谓的,Kenn-zeich-en 无关,un-ge-nannt 未提及,Ge-nannt-e 上述的人,Nenn-form 不定式(\"命名形\"),Nam-ens-vetter 同名者。",
    "nennen (to name) comes from Proto-Germanic *namnijaną — to give a name, the verb of Name; Name is PIE *h₁nómn̥, kin to Latin nomen, Greek onoma, English name, Sanskrit nāman. Nam-e a name, nam-ent-lich especially (by name), nam-haft renowned, Nam-ens-tag a name-day, nam-en-los nameless, Bei-nam-e an epithet, Vor-nam-e a first name, Nach-nam-e a surname, Deck-nam-e an alias, Spitz-nam-e a nickname, be-nenn-en to name, Be-nenn-ung a designation, er-nenn-en to appoint (to call by name), Er-nenn-ung an appointment, Nenn-er a denominator (the namer — a calque), Nenn-wert face value (nominal), Nenn-ung a nomination, so-ge-nannt so-called, un-ge-nannt unnamed, Ge-nannt-e the aforesaid, Nenn-form the infinitive (the naming form), Nam-ens-vetter a namesake."],
  fang: ["fangen(抓)来自原始日耳曼语 *fanhaną,印欧语 *peh₂ǵ-(固定、抓牢)——与拉丁语 pangere(固定)、pax(和平——\"定下的\")、pactum、希腊语 pēgnynai(固定)、英语 fang(獠牙——\"抓的东西\";古英语 fōn 抓)同根。Fang 捕获、獠牙,An-fang 开始(\"抓起\"——古高地德语 anafāhan 着手),an-fang-en 开始,An-fäng-er 初学者,an-fäng-lich 起初,Emp-fang 接待、接收(\"接住\"),emp-fang-en 接待、怀孕,Emp-fäng-er 收件人、接收器,Emp-fäng-nis 受孕(\"接住\"——conceptio 的直译),emp-fäng-lich 易感的,Ge-fang-ene 囚犯(\"被抓的\"),ge-fang-en 被俘,Ge-fäng-nis 监狱,Ge-fang-en-schaft 囚禁,Um-fang 范围(\"环抱\"——circumference),um-fang-reich 广泛,Ver-fang-en 缠住,fäng-t 抓,Fang-arm 触手,Wal-fang 捕鲸,Fisch-fang 捕鱼,Fang-frage 陷阱问题,Fang-zahn 獠牙,auf-fang-en 接住,Auf-fang-lager 收容营,ver-fäng-lich 尴尬的。",
    "fangen (to catch) comes from Proto-Germanic *fanhaną, PIE *peh₂ǵ- (to fasten, hold firm) — kin to Latin pangere (to fix), pax (peace — what is settled), pactum, Greek pēgnynai (to fix), English fang (a fang is the grasper; Old English fōn, to seize). Fang a catch, a fang, An-fang a beginning (taking hold — Old High German anafāhan, to set about), an-fang-en to begin, An-fäng-er a beginner, an-fäng-lich initially, Emp-fang a reception (a catching), emp-fang-en to receive, to conceive, Emp-fäng-er a recipient, a receiver, Emp-fäng-nis conception (a calque of conceptio), emp-fäng-lich susceptible, Ge-fang-ene a prisoner (the caught), ge-fang-en captured, Ge-fäng-nis a prison, Ge-fang-en-schaft captivity, Um-fang extent (what the arms embrace — circumference), um-fang-reich extensive, ver-fang-en to get caught, Fang-arm a tentacle, Wal-fang whaling, Fisch-fang fishing, Fang-frage a trick question, Fang-zahn a fang, auf-fang-en to catch, Auf-fang-lager a reception camp, ver-fäng-lich awkward."],
  treff: ["treffen(遇见、击中)来自原始日耳曼语 *drepaną(打),与古英语 drepan(打、杀)、荷兰语 treffen、古挪威语 drepa(杀)同根;印欧语 *dʰrebʰ-(打)存疑。德语的\"打中\"引申为\"遇到\"(碰上)和\"恰当\"(说中)。Treff-en 会面、集会,Treff-er 命中、中奖,Treff-punkt 会合点,treff-end 贴切的(\"击中的\"),vor-treff-lich 卓越的(\"超过命中\"),zu-treff-en 适用、属实,zu-treff-end 正确的,be-treff-en 涉及(\"击中\"——concern),Be-treff 事由(信件抬头),be-treff-s 关于,über-treff-en 超过,ein-treff-en 到达、应验,an-treff-en 碰见,Zusammen-treff-en 相遇、巧合,Voll-treff-er 正中,Treff-sicher 准确的,Treff 会面(口语),Fehl-treff-er 未中,ge-troff-en 被击中、遇见,Zu-sammen-treff-en 巧合,be-troff-en 受影响的、愕然。",
    "treffen (to meet, to hit) comes from Proto-Germanic *drepaną (to strike), kin to Old English drepan (to strike, kill), Dutch treffen, Old Norse drepa (to kill); PIE *dʰrebʰ- (to strike) is possible. German's hitting the mark grew into meeting (running into) and aptness (hitting it). Treff-en a meeting, a rally, Treff-er a hit, a win, Treff-punkt a meeting-point, treff-end apt (on target), vor-treff-lich excellent (beyond the mark), zu-treff-en to apply, to be true, zu-treff-end correct, be-treff-en to concern (to strike — concern), Be-treff the subject line, be-treff-s regarding, über-treff-en to surpass, ein-treff-en to arrive, to come true, an-treff-en to find someone in, Zusammen-treff-en an encounter, a coincidence, Voll-treff-er a direct hit, treff-sicher accurate, Treff a get-together, Fehl-treff-er a miss, ge-troff-en hit, met, be-troff-en affected, taken aback."],
  kenn: ["kennen(认识)与 können(能)同根:原始日耳曼语 *kannijaną(使知道)是 *kunnaną(知道、会)的使役式;印欧语 *ǵneh₃-(知)——与拉丁语 noscere、希腊语 gignōskein、英语 know、can(古义\"知道怎样\")、ken(苏格兰语\"知道\")、cunning(\"会的\")、uncouth(\"不认识的\")同根。Kennt-nis 知识(Kennt-nisse 复数),be-kannt 有名的、熟悉的(\"被认识的\"),Be-kannt-e 熟人,Be-kannt-schaft 相识,be-kannt-lich 众所周知,Be-kannt-mach-ung 公告,er-kenn-en 认出、认识到(\"认出来\"),Er-kennt-nis 认识、见解(康德的 Erkenntnis——cognition),an-er-kenn-en 承认(recognize),An-er-kenn-ung 承认、赞赏,Kenn-zeich-en 标志、车牌,kenn-zeich-nen 标示,Kenn-wort 口令,Kenn-er 行家,kenn-t-lich 可辨认的,ver-kenn-en 误解,un-kennt-lich 无法辨认,Er-kenn-ung 识别(Spracherkennung 语音识别),Kön-ig 国王(\"出身的\"——kin 的亲戚,不是 können),Kund-e 顾客(\"熟人\"——古义认识的人),kund 知晓,Kund-e 消息,er-kund-en 探查,Ur-kund-e 证书(\"原初的知会\"),kund-ig 内行,Kunst 艺术(\"会的东西\"——können 的名词!)。",
    "kennen (to know) shares a root with können (to be able): Proto-Germanic *kannijaną (to make known) is the causative of *kunnaną (to know, to know how); PIE *ǵneh₃- (to know) — kin to Latin noscere, Greek gignōskein, English know, can (once to know how), ken (Scots), cunning (knowing), uncouth (unknown). Kennt-nis knowledge (plural Kennt-nisse), be-kannt famous, familiar (known), Be-kannt-e an acquaintance, Be-kannt-schaft acquaintance, be-kannt-lich as is well known, Be-kannt-mach-ung an announcement, er-kenn-en to recognise, to realise, Er-kennt-nis insight (Kant's Erkenntnis — cognition), an-er-kenn-en to acknowledge, An-er-kenn-ung recognition, Kenn-zeich-en a mark, a number plate, kenn-zeich-nen to characterise, Kenn-wort a password, Kenn-er a connoisseur, kenn-t-lich recognisable, ver-kenn-en to misjudge, un-kennt-lich unrecognisable, Er-kenn-ung recognition (Spracherkennung, speech recognition), Kund-e a customer (once one who is known), kund known, Kund-e news, er-kund-en to explore, Ur-kund-e a document (the original making-known), kund-ig expert, and Kunst art (what one can do — the noun of können!); Kön-ig, king, is kin to kin, not to können."],
  kind: ["Kind(孩子)来自原始日耳曼语 *kinþą(生出的),印欧语 *ǵenh₁-(生)——与拉丁语 gens、genus、gignere、希腊语 genos、英语 kin、kind(种类;kind 善良是\"同族般的\")、king(\"族人之首\")同根;所以德语的 Kind 与英语的 kin、kind、king、拉丁语的 gene、nation 是一家。Kind-heit 童年,kind-lich 孩子般的(童真),kind-isch 幼稚的(贬),Kind-er-garten 幼儿园(福禄贝尔 1840 年造——进了英语),Kind-er-arzt 儿科医生,Kind-er-buch 童书,Kind-er-geld 儿童津贴,Kind-es-alter 童年,Enkel-kind 孙辈,Wunsch-kind 期盼的孩子,Klein-kind 幼儿,Ge-schwister-kind 表亲(古义),Kind-s-kopf 孩子气的人,Kinds-kind 曾孙,Kind-bett 产褥,Kind-er-reich 多子的,Kön-ig 国王(同根!\"出身高贵者\"),Kind-e-s-wohl 儿童福祉,Christ-kind 圣婴。",
    "Kind (a child) comes from Proto-Germanic *kinþą (the begotten), PIE *ǵenh₁- (to beget) — kin to Latin gens, genus, gignere, Greek genos, English kin, kind (a sort; kind as gentle is \"like one's kin\"), king (head of the kin); so German Kind is family to English kin, kind, king and Latin gene, nation. Kind-heit childhood, kind-lich childlike, kind-isch childish, Kind-er-garten (Fröbel's 1840 coinage, borrowed into English), Kind-er-arzt a paediatrician, Kind-er-buch a children's book, Kind-er-geld child benefit, Kind-es-alter childhood, Enkel-kind a grandchild, Wunsch-kind a wanted child, Klein-kind a toddler, Ge-schwister-kind a cousin (old usage), Kind-s-kopf a big child, Kinds-kind a great-grandchild, Kind-bett childbed, kind-er-reich with many children, Kön-ig a king (same root — the well-born), Kind-e-s-wohl a child's welfare, Christ-kind the Christ child."],
  tod: ["Tod(死)来自原始日耳曼语 *dauþuz,是 *dawjaną(死)的名词;与英语 death、dead、die(经古挪世语 deyja)、荷兰语 dood、哥特语 dauþus 同根;印欧语 *dʰew-(消散、死)——与拉丁语 funus(葬礼)?存疑、古爱尔兰语 duine(人——\"会死的\")、亚美尼亚语 di(尸体)同源。tot 死的,töt-en 杀(\"使死\"),tot-al 无关,Tod-es-strafe 死刑,Tod-es-angst 死亡恐惧,tod-krank 病危,tod-müde 累死,tod-ernst 极严肃,tod-sicher 万无一失(\"死也确定\"),Tod-feind 死敌,Tod-sünde 大罪,Tod-es-fall 死亡事件,Tod-es-anzeige 讣告,Tot-e 死者,Tot-en-kopf 骷髅,Toten-tanz 死亡之舞(中世纪壁画),Un-tot-e 不死者,Tot-schlag 过失杀人(\"打死\"),Tot-en-stille 死寂,tot-lachen 笑死,Tod-es-urteil 死刑判决,Tot-geburt 死产,ab-töt-en 杀灭,er-töt-en?否,Tod-es-mut 视死如归。",
    "Tod (death) comes from Proto-Germanic *dauþuz, the noun of *dawjaną (to die); kin to English death, dead, die (via Old Norse deyja), Dutch dood, Gothic dauþus; PIE *dʰew- (to fade, die) — with Old Irish duine (a human — the mortal) and Armenian di (a corpse). tot dead, töt-en to kill (to make dead), Tod-es-strafe the death penalty, Tod-es-angst mortal fear, tod-krank mortally ill, tod-müde dead tired, tod-ernst deadly serious, tod-sicher dead certain, Tod-feind a mortal enemy, Tod-sünde a deadly sin, Tod-es-fall a death, Tod-es-anzeige an obituary, Tot-e the dead, Tot-en-kopf a skull, Toten-tanz the Dance of Death (medieval murals), Un-tot-e the undead, Tot-schlag manslaughter (striking dead), Tot-en-stille dead silence, tot-lachen to die laughing, Tod-es-urteil a death sentence, Tot-geburt a stillbirth, ab-töt-en to kill off, Tod-es-mut reckless courage."],
  gott: ["Gott(神)来自原始日耳曼语 *gudą,印欧语 *ǵʰu-tó-(\"被呼唤的\"或\"被奠酒的\")——是 *ǵʰeu-(呼唤,梵语 hávate 呼唤)或 *ǵʰeu-(倒,希腊语 kheein)的分词;英语 god、荷兰语 god、哥特语 guþ 同根,与拉丁语 deus 无关。日耳曼语的 *gudą 原是中性(\"被崇拜的东西\"),基督教后变为阳性。Gött-in 女神,gött-lich 神圣的,Gott-heit 神性,Gott-es-dienst 礼拜(\"事神\"),Gott-es-haus 教堂,gott-los 无神的、邪恶,Gott-es-furcht 敬畏神,Ab-gott 偶像(\"离开神的\"),ab-gött-isch 盲目崇拜,ver-gött-ern 崇拜,Gott-es-lästerung 亵渎,gott-er-geben 听天由命,Grüß Gott 南德问候(\"愿神问候你\"),Gott-vater 圣父,Halb-gott 半神,Gött-er-dämmerung 诸神的黄昏(瓦格纳译 Ragnarök),Gott-es-gnaden-tum 君权神授,gott-ver-lassen 荒僻,Gott-lob 谢天谢地,Gott-vertrauen 信靠神。",
    "Gott (God) comes from Proto-Germanic *gudą, PIE *ǵʰu-tó- (\"the invoked\" or \"the one libated to\") — the participle of *ǵʰeu- (to call; Sanskrit hávate) or *ǵʰeu- (to pour; Greek kheein); English god, Dutch god, Gothic guþ are kin, Latin deus is not. Germanic *gudą was originally neuter (the thing worshipped) and became masculine with Christianity. Gött-in a goddess, gött-lich divine, Gott-heit deity, Gott-es-dienst a church service (God-service), Gott-es-haus a church, gott-los godless, wicked, Gott-es-furcht the fear of God, Ab-gott an idol (away from God), ab-gött-isch idolatrous, ver-gött-ern to idolise, Gott-es-lästerung blasphemy, gott-er-geben resigned to fate, Grüß Gott the southern greeting (may God greet you), Gott-vater God the Father, Halb-gott a demigod, Gött-er-dämmerung the twilight of the gods (Wagner's Ragnarök), Gott-es-gnaden-tum divine right, gott-ver-lassen godforsaken, Gott-lob thank God, Gott-vertrauen trust in God."],
  geist: ["Geist(精神、幽灵)来自原始日耳曼语 *gaistaz(激动、恐惧、幽灵),与英语 ghost(古英语 gāst——Holy Ghost 圣灵)、荷兰语 geest 同根;印欧语 *ǵʰeis-(激动、惊恐),与哥特语 usgaisjan(惊吓——aghast 是它的英语残迹)、梵语 hedah(愤怒)同源。它在德语里从\"幽灵\"扩展为\"心智\"(译拉丁语 spiritus 和 mens):Heil-iger Geist 圣灵,Geist-es-wissenschaften 人文科学(\"精神科学\",狄尔泰 1883),Zeit-geist 时代精神(黑格尔时代,进了英语),Welt-geist 世界精神,geist-ig 精神的、智力的,geist-lich 宗教的,Geist-lich-er 神职人员,geist-reich 机智的(\"富于精神\"),Geist-es-blitz 灵光一现,Geist-es-krankheit 精神病,be-geist-ern 使热情(\"注入精神\"——inspire),Be-geist-erung 热情,Geist-er-bahn 鬼屋列车,Geist-er-stadt 鬼城,geist-es-abwesend 心不在焉,Polter-geist 吵闹鬼(进了英语),Frei-geist 自由思想者,Geist-es-gegenwart 镇定(\"精神在场\"——présence d'esprit)。",
    "Geist (spirit, ghost) comes from Proto-Germanic *gaistaz (agitation, terror, a ghost), kin to English ghost (Old English gāst — Holy Ghost), Dutch geest; PIE *ǵʰeis- (to be agitated, frightened), with Gothic usgaisjan (to terrify — aghast is its English relic) and Sanskrit hedah (anger). German widened it from ghost to mind (rendering Latin spiritus and mens): Heil-iger Geist the Holy Spirit, Geist-es-wissenschaften the humanities (sciences of the spirit, Dilthey 1883), Zeit-geist the spirit of the age (Hegel's era, borrowed into English), Welt-geist the world-spirit, geist-ig mental, intellectual, geist-lich religious, Geist-lich-er a clergyman, geist-reich witty (rich in spirit), Geist-es-blitz a flash of inspiration, Geist-es-krankheit mental illness, be-geist-ern to inspire (to fill with spirit), Be-geist-erung enthusiasm, Geist-er-bahn a ghost train, Geist-er-stadt a ghost town, geist-es-abwesend absent-minded, Polter-geist (borrowed into English), Frei-geist a freethinker, Geist-es-gegenwart presence of mind."],
  mann: ["Mann(男人)来自原始日耳曼语 *mannz(人),印欧语 *man-(人)——与梵语 manu(人类始祖 Manu)、俄语 muzh(丈夫)、英语 man 同根;塔西佗记载日耳曼人的始祖叫 Mannus。它先指\"人\"(不分性别——man 至今如此;德语 man 不定代词\"人们\"就是它的残留),后专指男人。Mann-schaft 团队(\"一群人\"),männ-lich 男性的、阳性的,Mann-heit?否,Männ-chen 雄性动物,Ehe-mann 丈夫,Kauf-mann 商人(复数 Kaufleute——Leute 人们),Haupt-mann 上尉(\"头人\"),Land-mann 农民,Amt-mann 官员,Fach-mann 专家,Staats-mann 政治家,Schnee-mann 雪人,Jeder-mann 每个人(中世纪道德剧《Everyman》的德译),Nie-mand 无人(ni-io-man),Je-mand 某人,Mann-es-alter 壮年,be-mann-en 配备船员,Mann-bar-keit 成年,Herr-en-mensch?否,Ober-mann?否,Wer-wolf 狼人(wer 是另一个\"人\"字——拉丁 vir)。",
    "Mann (a man) comes from Proto-Germanic *mannz (a human), PIE *man- (person) — kin to Sanskrit manu (Manu, ancestor of mankind), Russian muzh (husband), English man; Tacitus records the Germanic ancestor-god Mannus. It first meant a person of either sex (English man still can; the German indefinite pronoun man, \"one\", is its relic), then a male. Mann-schaft a team (a body of men), männ-lich male, masculine, Männ-chen a male animal, Ehe-mann a husband, Kauf-mann a merchant (plural Kaufleute — Leute people), Haupt-mann a captain (head-man), Land-mann a countryman, Amt-mann an official, Fach-mann an expert, Staats-mann a statesman, Schnee-mann a snowman, Jeder-mann everyman (the German title of the morality play), Nie-mand nobody (ni-io-man), Je-mand somebody, Mann-es-alter manhood, be-mann-en to man, Mann-bar-keit maturity; Wer-wolf holds the other word for man, wer — Latin vir."],
  volk: ["Volk(人民)来自原始日耳曼语 *fulką(军队、人群),与英语 folk、荷兰语 volk、古挪威语 folk(军队)同根;印欧语 *pl̥h₁-go-(填满的——一大群),与 voll、full、拉丁语 plebs、populus?(同根 *pleh₁-)同源:人民就是\"满满一群\"。Volk 在 19 世纪民族主义里成了核心词(赫尔德的 Volksgeist),20 世纪被纳粹滥用(völkisch),战后德语小心使用。Be-völk-erung 人口(\"住满的人\"),be-völk-ern 居住,ent-völk-ern 使人口减少,Volks-lied 民歌(赫尔德 1773 年造——folksong 由此),Volks-wagen 大众汽车(1937),Volks-hoch-schule 成人教育学校,Volks-wirt-schaft 国民经济,Volks-tanz 民间舞,Volks-fest 民俗节,Volks-kunde 民俗学,Völker-recht 国际法(\"民族之法\"——ius gentium 的直译),Völker-wanderung 民族大迁徙,Völker-mord 种族灭绝(genocide 的德译),Volks-ab-stimmung 公投,Volks-vertreter 人民代表,völk-isch 民族主义的(贬),volks-tüm-lich 通俗的,Volks-mund 民间说法,Volks-zählung 人口普查。",
    "Volk (a people) comes from Proto-Germanic *fulką (a host, a crowd), kin to English folk, Dutch volk, Old Norse folk (an army); PIE *pl̥h₁-go- (filled up — a throng), with voll, full, and Latin plebs (root *pleh₁-): a people is a full crowd. Volk became the key word of 19th-century nationalism (Herder's Volksgeist) and was abused by the Nazis (völkisch), so post-war German handles it with care. Be-völk-erung population (those filling a place), be-völk-ern to populate, ent-völk-ern to depopulate, Volks-lied a folk-song (Herder's 1773 coinage — whence folksong), Volks-wagen the people's car (1937), Volks-hoch-schule adult education, Volks-wirt-schaft the national economy, Volks-tanz folk dance, Volks-fest a fair, Volks-kunde folklore studies, Völker-recht international law (the law of peoples — a calque of ius gentium), Völker-wanderung the Migration Period, Völker-mord genocide, Volks-ab-stimmung a referendum, Volks-vertreter a representative, völk-isch ethnic-nationalist (pejorative), volks-tüm-lich popular, Volks-mund the vernacular, Volks-zählung a census."],
  fried: ["Friede(和平)来自原始日耳曼语 *friþuz(和平、保护),是 *frijaz(自由的、亲爱的——frei)的派生:和平是\"亲族之间的互爱\";印欧语 *priH-(爱)——与梵语 priya(亲爱的)、英语 free、friend(\"爱的人\")、Friday(爱神 Frigg 之日)、哥特语 frijōn(爱)同根。中世纪的 Landfriede 是禁止私斗的和约;Fried-hof 墓地(\"围起的院子\"——这个 fried 其实是 einfrieden 围篱,与和平混同),fried-lich 和平的,fried-fertig 爱好和平,Fried-ens-vertrag 和约,Fried-ens-nobel-preis 诺贝尔和平奖,zu-fried-en 满意的(\"处于和平\"),Zu-fried-en-heit 满足,un-zu-fried-en 不满,be-fried-en 平定,be-fried-ig-en 满足(需求),Be-fried-ig-ung 满足,Un-fried-e 不和,Fried-rich 弗里德里希(\"和平之王\"——fried + rīki 统治),Sieg-fried 齐格弗里德(\"胜利与和平\"),Gott-fried,Ruhe in Frieden 安息,Frieden-s-taube 和平鸽,Fried-ens-schluss 缔和。",
    "Friede (peace) comes from Proto-Germanic *friþuz (peace, protection), derived from *frijaz (free, dear — frei): peace is the mutual love of kin; PIE *priH- (to love) — kin to Sanskrit priya (dear), English free, friend (the loved one), Friday (the day of the love-goddess Frigg), Gothic frijōn (to love). The medieval Landfriede was a truce banning private feuds; Fried-hof a cemetery (an enclosed yard — this fried is really einfrieden, to fence, merged with peace), fried-lich peaceful, fried-fertig peaceable, Fried-ens-vertrag a peace treaty, Fried-ens-nobel-preis the Nobel Peace Prize, zu-fried-en content (at peace), Zu-fried-en-heit contentment, un-zu-fried-en dissatisfied, be-fried-en to pacify, be-fried-ig-en to satisfy, Be-fried-ig-ung satisfaction, Un-fried-e discord, Fried-rich Frederick (peace-ruler — fried + rīki), Sieg-fried (victory-peace), Gott-fried, Ruhe in Frieden rest in peace, Frieden-s-taube the dove of peace, Fried-ens-schluss a peace settlement."],
  herr: ["Herr(先生、主人)是古高地德语 hēriro——hēr(尊贵的、灰发的)的比较级:\"更尊贵的人\",仿拉丁语 senior(更老的)造出;hēr 与英语 hoar(灰白)、hoary 同根,印欧语 *ḱei-(灰)。所以 Herr 与 sir、señor、monsieur 的逻辑一样:年长者受尊敬。基督教用 Herr 译 Dominus(主),Herr-gott 上帝;Herr-in 女主人,herr-lich 壮丽的(\"主人般的\"),Herr-lich-keit 荣耀,herr-schen 统治,Herr-schaft 统治、领地(Herrschaften 各位),Herr-scher 统治者,be-herr-schen 掌握、控制,Be-herr-schung 掌握、自制,herr-isch 专横,Herr-en-haus 庄园主宅,Herr-en-mode 男装,Alleinherr-schaft 独裁,Vor-herr-schaft 霸权,Herr-en-los 无主的,mein Herr 先生,Herr-schafts-zeiten! 天哪,Ober-herr 宗主,Land-es-herr 领主,ver-herr-lichen 颂扬。",
    "Herr (Mr, master) is Old High German hēriro — the comparative of hēr (venerable, grey-haired): \"the more venerable one\", modelled on Latin senior (the elder); hēr is kin to English hoar and hoary, PIE *ḱei- (grey). So Herr follows the same logic as sir, señor, monsieur: the elder is honoured. The Church used Herr for Dominus, the Lord, Herr-gott God; Herr-in a mistress, herr-lich magnificent (lordly), Herr-lich-keit glory, herr-schen to rule, Herr-schaft rule, a domain (Herrschaften ladies and gentlemen), Herr-scher a ruler, be-herr-schen to master, to control, Be-herr-schung mastery, self-control, herr-isch imperious, Herr-en-haus a manor house, Herr-en-mode menswear, Alleinherr-schaft autocracy, Vor-herr-schaft hegemony, herr-en-los ownerless, mein Herr sir, Herr-schafts-zeiten! good heavens, Ober-herr an overlord, Land-es-herr a territorial lord, ver-herr-lichen to glorify."],
  folg: ["folgen(跟随)来自西日耳曼语 *fulgijaną,与英语 follow(古英语 folgian)、荷兰语 volgen 同根;词源不明——可能与 *fulla-gangan(\"走满\")的缩合有关(存疑)。Folg-e 后果、集(电视剧一集)、顺序(Reihen-folge 次序),folg-lich 因此,in-folge 由于,in-folge-dessen 因此,Er-folg 成功(\"跟着来的结果\"——16 世纪从中性\"结果\"转为好结果),er-folg-reich 成功的,er-folg-los 徒劳,Miss-erfolg 失败,Ge-folg-e 随从(\"跟着的人\"),Ge-folg-schaft 追随者,ver-folg-en 追踪、迫害,Ver-folg-ung 迫害,Ver-folg-er 追捕者,be-folg-en 遵守(指示),Be-folg-ung 遵从,nach-folg-en 继任,Nach-folg-er 继任者,Nach-folg-e 继承,Folg-er-ung 推论,folg-ern 推断,Schluss-folg-er-ung 结论,folg-sam 听话的,Zeit-folg-e 时序,Ab-folg-e 顺序,Folg-e-kosten 后续费用,ver-folg-bar 可追踪的。",
    "folgen (to follow) comes from West Germanic *fulgijaną, kin to English follow (Old English folgian), Dutch volgen; its origin is uncertain — possibly a contraction of *fulla-gangan (to go in full). Folg-e a consequence, an episode, a sequence (Reihen-folge order), folg-lich consequently, in-folge owing to, in-folge-dessen as a result, Er-folg success (what follows — a neutral \"result\" until the 16th century), er-folg-reich successful, er-folg-los fruitless, Miss-erfolg failure, Ge-folg-e a retinue (those who follow), Ge-folg-schaft followers, ver-folg-en to pursue, to persecute, Ver-folg-ung persecution, Ver-folg-er a pursuer, be-folg-en to follow (instructions), Be-folg-ung compliance, nach-folg-en to succeed, Nach-folg-er a successor, Nach-folg-e succession, Folg-er-ung an inference, folg-ern to infer, Schluss-folg-er-ung a conclusion, folg-sam obedient, Zeit-folg-e chronology, Ab-folg-e a sequence, Folg-e-kosten follow-on costs, ver-folg-bar traceable."],
  gleich: ["gleich(相同的)是古高地德语 gilīh 的缩合:ga-(一起)+ līka(身体、形状——Leiche 尸体、英语 like、-ly);\"有同一个形体的\"。英语 like、alike(古英语 gelīc)是同一个词;-lich/-ly 后缀也是这个\"形体\"(kind-ly 有…的样子)。gleich 也作副词\"马上\"(\"同时\");Gleich-heit 平等(Liberté, Égalité 的德译),gleich-en 相像,ver-gleich-en 比较(\"使相同\"),Ver-gleich 比较、和解,ver-gleich-bar 可比的,un-ver-gleich-lich 无与伦比,aus-gleich-en 平衡,Aus-gleich 补偿、平衡,Gleich-ge-wicht 平衡(\"同重\"——aequilibrium 的直译),gleich-gültig 冷漠的(\"一样有效\"→无所谓),Gleich-gültig-keit 冷漠,gleich-zeitig 同时,gleich-mäßig 均匀,Gleich-ung 方程(\"使相等\"),Gleich-nis 比喻(路德译 parabola),gleich-berechtigt 平权,Gleich-strom 直流电,gleich-falls 同样,gleich-sam 仿佛,Gleich-schaltung 一体化(纳粹术语),an-gleich-en 使适应,be-gleich-en 结清,gleich-wohl 尽管如此,Leich-e 尸体(\"形体\"),Leich-nam 尸身。",
    "gleich (same, equal) is Old High German gilīh contracted: ga- (together) + līka (body, shape — Leiche a corpse, English like, -ly): having one and the same shape. English like and alike (Old English gelīc) are the same word, and the suffix -lich/-ly is this \"shape\" too (kind-ly, in the shape of kindness). gleich as an adverb means at once (at the same moment); Gleich-heit equality (the German of Égalité), gleich-en to resemble, ver-gleich-en to compare (to make equal), Ver-gleich a comparison, a settlement, ver-gleich-bar comparable, un-ver-gleich-lich incomparable, aus-gleich-en to balance, Aus-gleich compensation, balance, Gleich-ge-wicht equilibrium (equal weight — a calque of aequilibrium), gleich-gültig indifferent (equally valid → all the same), Gleich-gültig-keit indifference, gleich-zeitig simultaneous, gleich-mäßig even, Gleich-ung an equation (a making equal), Gleich-nis a parable (Luther's word for parabola), gleich-berechtigt with equal rights, Gleich-strom direct current, gleich-falls likewise, gleich-sam as it were, Gleich-schaltung enforced conformity (a Nazi term), an-gleich-en to align, be-gleich-en to settle (a bill), gleich-wohl nevertheless, Leich-e a corpse (a body), Leich-nam a dead body."],
  eigen: ["eigen(自己的)是原始日耳曼语 *aiganaz——*aiganą(拥有;英语 owe、own、ought——owe 本义\"拥有\")的分词:\"被拥有的\";印欧语 *h₂eyḱ-(拥有),与梵语 īśe(拥有、统治——Īśvara 主宰)、阿维斯陀语 iš- 同根。Eigen-schaft 特性(\"自己的状态\"——proprietas 的直译),Eigen-tum 财产(property),Eigen-tüm-er 所有者,eigen-tüm-lich 奇特的(\"特有的\"),eigen-t-lich 其实、本来(\"按本性说\"),Eigen-art 特色,eigen-artig 奇怪,Eigen-name 专名,Eigen-sinn 固执(\"自己的心\"),eigen-sinn-ig 任性,Eigen-nutz 私利,eigen-nütz-ig 自私,Eigen-wert 内在价值、特征值(数学 eigenvalue 直接借德语!),Eigen-vektor 特征向量,Eigen-liebe 自恋,eigen-händig 亲手,eigen-mächtig 擅自,Eigen-ver-antwort-ung 自我负责,sich an-eign-en 占为己有、掌握,An-eign-ung 占有、习得,ent-eign-en 征收,Ent-eign-ung 没收,ge-eign-et 合适的(\"归属的\"),Eign-ung 适合性,Eigen-heim 自有住房,Eigen-brötler 独行者(\"自己烤面包的人\")。",
    "eigen (own) is Proto-Germanic *aiganaz — the participle of *aiganą (to possess; English owe, own, ought — owe first meant to possess): \"possessed\"; PIE *h₂eyḱ- (to own), kin to Sanskrit īśe (to own, rule — Īśvara the Lord) and Avestan iš-. Eigen-schaft a property, a quality (one's own state — a calque of proprietas), Eigen-tum property, Eigen-tüm-er an owner, eigen-tüm-lich peculiar (proper to), eigen-t-lich actually, really (by its own nature), Eigen-art a peculiarity, eigen-artig strange, Eigen-name a proper name, Eigen-sinn obstinacy (one's own mind), eigen-sinn-ig headstrong, Eigen-nutz self-interest, eigen-nütz-ig selfish, Eigen-wert intrinsic value, and the eigenvalue (mathematics borrowed the German word whole!), Eigen-vektor an eigenvector, Eigen-liebe self-love, eigen-händig with one's own hand, eigen-mächtig high-handed, Eigen-ver-antwort-ung personal responsibility, sich an-eign-en to appropriate, to acquire (a skill), An-eign-ung appropriation, acquisition, ent-eign-en to expropriate, Ent-eign-ung expropriation, ge-eign-et suitable (belonging to), Eign-ung aptitude, Eigen-heim one's own home, Eigen-brötler a loner (one who bakes his own bread)."],
  drück: ["drücken(压)来自原始日耳曼语 *þrukkijaną(推、压),与英语 throng(挤的人群——古英语 þringan 挤)、荷兰语 drukken、古挪威语 þrykkja 同根;印欧语 *trenk-(推、挤)——与立陶宛语 treñkti(撞)、拉丁语 truncus?存疑同源。Druck 压力、印刷(15 世纪古腾堡的\"压印\"——印刷术是 drucken\"压\",所以 Buch-druck 印刷术、Druck-er 印刷工/打印机、Druck-erei 印刷厂),drück-en 压、拥抱,aus-drück-en 表达、挤出(\"压出来\"——exprimere 的直译),Aus-druck 表达、词语(expression),aus-drück-lich 明确地,be-drück-en 使沮丧,be-drück-t 压抑的,unter-drück-en 压制(opprimere),Unter-drück-ung 压迫,ein-drück-en 压进,Ein-druck 印象(impressio 的直译),be-ein-druck-en 给人印象,ein-drucks-voll 令人难忘,ab-druck-en 刊印,Ab-druck 印痕、复制,Nach-druck 强调、重印,nach-drück-lich 强调地,Blut-druck 血压,Über-druck 超压,Druck-mittel 施压手段,Hand-druck 握手,drück-end 闷热的,Zu-drück-en 按下,Ge-dränge 拥挤(dringen 同根),dring-end 紧急(\"挤进来的\")。",
    "drücken (to press) comes from Proto-Germanic *þrukkijaną (to push, press), kin to English throng (the pressing crowd — Old English þringan, to press), Dutch drukken, Old Norse þrykkja; PIE *trenk- (to push, crowd), with Lithuanian treñkti (to strike). Druck pressure, and printing (Gutenberg's 15th-century press — printing is drucken, pressing, so Buch-druck typography, Druck-er a printer, Druck-erei a print shop), drück-en to press, to hug, aus-drück-en to express, to squeeze out (a calque of exprimere), Aus-druck an expression, a term, aus-drück-lich expressly, be-drück-en to depress, be-drück-t downcast, unter-drück-en to suppress (opprimere), Unter-drück-ung oppression, ein-drück-en to press in, Ein-druck an impression (a calque of impressio), be-ein-druck-en to impress, ein-drucks-voll impressive, ab-druck-en to print, Ab-druck an imprint, a reprint, Nach-druck emphasis, a reprint, nach-drück-lich emphatically, Blut-druck blood pressure, Über-druck overpressure, Druck-mittel leverage, Hand-druck a handshake, drück-end oppressive (heat), Ge-dränge a crush (dringen shares the root), dring-end urgent (pressing in)."],
  red: ["Rede(话)来自原始日耳曼语 *raþjō(计算、说明),与哥特语 raþjō(数、账)、英语 无直接(read 是另一分支)、荷兰语 rede(理性)同根;印欧语 *reh₁-(思量、计算)——与拉丁语 ratio、reri、德语 Rat、raten、英语 read 同源:说话就是\"把道理数出来\"(荷兰语 rede 至今是\"理性\")。Red-e 演讲、话语,red-en 说话,Red-ner 演说家,Red-e-wend-ung 习语(\"话的转法\"),Red-e-frei-heit 言论自由,An-red-e 称呼,an-red-en 招呼,Aus-red-e 借口(\"说出去的话\"),Ab-red-e 约定,ab-red-en 劝阻,ver-ab-red-en 约定,Ver-ab-red-ung 约会,Ge-red-e 闲话,über-red-en 说服,Über-red-ung 劝说,unter-red-en 交谈,Unter-red-ung 会谈,Vor-red-e 序言,Nach-red-e 诽谤(üble Nachrede),be-red-t 能言的,Be-red-sam-keit 口才(eloquentia 的直译),Red-ens-art 说法,Red-e-fluss 滔滔不绝,zur Rede stellen 质问,red-lich 诚实的(\"说得清账的\"),Red-lich-keit 正直,Recht-fertig-ung 无关。",
    "Rede (speech) comes from Proto-Germanic *raþjō (a reckoning, an account), kin to Gothic raþjō (a number, an account), Dutch rede (reason); PIE *reh₁- (to think, count) — with Latin ratio, reri, German Rat, raten, English read: to speak is to count out one's reasons (Dutch rede still means reason). Red-e a speech, talk, red-en to speak, Red-ner an orator, Red-e-wend-ung an idiom (a turn of speech), Red-e-frei-heit free speech, An-red-e a form of address, an-red-en to address, Aus-red-e an excuse (talk to get out), Ab-red-e an agreement, ab-red-en to dissuade, ver-ab-red-en to arrange, Ver-ab-red-ung an appointment, Ge-red-e gossip, über-red-en to persuade, Über-red-ung persuasion, unter-red-en to confer, Unter-red-ung a discussion, Vor-red-e a preface, Nach-red-e slander (üble Nachrede), be-red-t eloquent, Be-red-sam-keit eloquence (a calque of eloquentia), Red-ens-art a turn of phrase, Red-e-fluss a flow of words, zur Rede stellen to call to account, red-lich honest (able to give a clear account), Red-lich-keit integrity."],
  deck: ["decken(盖)来自原始日耳曼语 *þakjaną(盖顶),是 *þaką(屋顶——Dach、英语 thatch)的动词;印欧语 *(s)teg-(盖)——与拉丁语 tegere、tectum、toga、希腊语 stegos(屋顶)、英语 deck(甲板,经荷兰语 dek)同根。Deck-e 天花板、毯子、被子(\"盖的\"),Deck-el 盖子,Dach 屋顶,ent-deck-en 发现(\"揭盖\"——detegere/découvrir 的直译),Ent-deck-ung 发现,Ent-deck-er 发现者(Kolumbus),be-deck-en 覆盖,be-deck-t 阴天的、覆盖的,Be-deck-ung 遮盖、护卫,ver-deck-en 遮掩,ver-deck-t 隐蔽的,Ver-deck 车篷,auf-deck-en 揭露,Auf-deck-ung 揭发,ab-deck-en 盖住、涵盖,Ab-deck-ung 覆盖范围,zu-deck-en 盖好,Deck-ung 掩护、保障,deck-ungs-gleich 全等(几何),Deck-name 代号,Deck-mantel 幌子,Ge-deck 一套餐具(\"铺的\"),Tisch-deck-e 桌布,Bett-deck-e 被子,Dach-boden 阁楼,Dach-s?否(獾是另一个词),Ober-deck 上层甲板,Deck-blatt 封面页。",
    "decken (to cover) comes from Proto-Germanic *þakjaną (to roof), the verb of *þaką (a roof — Dach, English thatch); PIE *(s)teg- (to cover) — kin to Latin tegere, tectum, toga, Greek stegos (a roof), English deck (via Dutch dek). Deck-e a ceiling, a blanket, a duvet (the cover), Deck-el a lid, Dach a roof, ent-deck-en to discover (to uncover — a calque of detegere/découvrir), Ent-deck-ung a discovery, Ent-deck-er a discoverer (Kolumbus), be-deck-en to cover, be-deck-t overcast, covered, Be-deck-ung covering, an escort, ver-deck-en to conceal, ver-deck-t covert, Ver-deck a car's hood, auf-deck-en to expose, Auf-deck-ung exposure, ab-deck-en to cover, to cover (a topic), Ab-deck-ung coverage, zu-deck-en to tuck in, Deck-ung cover, security, deck-ungs-gleich congruent, Deck-name a code name, Deck-mantel a pretext, Ge-deck a place setting (what is laid), Tisch-deck-e a tablecloth, Bett-deck-e a duvet, Dach-boden an attic, Ober-deck the upper deck, Deck-blatt a cover sheet."],
  schad: ["Schade(损害)来自原始日耳曼语 *skaþô(伤害、害人者),与英语 scathe(经古挪威语——unscathed 毫发无损)、荷兰语 schade、哥特语 skaþis 同根;印欧语 *skēth-(伤害),与希腊语 a-skēthēs(无伤)、古爱尔兰语 scathaim(损坏)同源。schad-e 可惜(\"这是损失\"),Schad-en 损失、损坏,schad-en 伤害,Schad-en-ersatz 赔偿,Schad-en-freude 幸灾乐祸(进了英语),schäd-lich 有害的,Schäd-ling 害虫,schäd-ig-en 损害,Schäd-ig-ung 损害,be-schäd-ig-en 损坏,Be-schäd-ig-ung 损坏,ent-schäd-ig-en 补偿,Ent-schäd-ig-ung 赔偿,un-schäd-lich 无害,Schad-stoff 有害物质,schad-los 无损(sich schadlos halten 补偿自己),Schad-en-fall 保险事故,Sach-schad-en 财产损失,Total-schad-en 全损,Schad-en-begrenzung 止损,ver-schad?否,Schäd-el(头骨)是另一个词。",
    "Schade (harm) comes from Proto-Germanic *skaþô (injury, an injurer), kin to English scathe (via Old Norse — unscathed), Dutch schade, Gothic skaþis; PIE *skēth- (to harm), with Greek a-skēthēs (unharmed), Old Irish scathaim (to damage). schad-e what a pity (it is a loss), Schad-en damage, schad-en to harm, Schad-en-ersatz compensation, Schad-en-freude malicious joy (borrowed into English), schäd-lich harmful, Schäd-ling a pest, schäd-ig-en to damage, Schäd-ig-ung damage, be-schäd-ig-en to damage, Be-schäd-ig-ung damage, ent-schäd-ig-en to compensate, Ent-schäd-ig-ung compensation, un-schäd-lich harmless, Schad-stoff a pollutant, schad-los unharmed (sich schadlos halten to recoup oneself), Schad-en-fall an insurance claim, Sach-schad-en property damage, Total-schad-en a write-off, Schad-en-begrenzung damage limitation; Schäd-el (skull) is a different word."],
  werb: ["werben(招募、宣传)来自原始日耳曼语 *hwerbaną(转、绕),与英语 whirl?否——与古英语 hweorfan(转、去)、荷兰语 werven、哥特语 hvairban(行走)同根;印欧语 *kʷerp-(转)——与希腊语 karpos(手腕——转的地方)、英语 wharf?(存疑)同源。中世纪的 werben 是\"来回奔走\"——为招兵、求婚、拉生意奔走,于是有了三个现代义:Werb-ung 广告(20 世纪从\"招徕\"来)、求婚(um jemanden werben)、招募(An-werb-ung),Werb-e-spot 广告片,Werb-e-agentur 广告公司,Be-werb-ung 申请(\"为自己奔走\"),sich be-werb-en 申请,Be-werb-er 申请人,Wett-be-werb 竞争(\"比着奔走\"),Wett-be-werb-er 竞争者,Er-werb 获得、生计(\"奔走所得\"),er-werb-en 获得,Er-werbs-los 无业,er-werbs-tätig 就业的,Ge-werb-e 行业(\"营生\"),ge-werb-lich 商业的,Ge-werk-schaft 无关(Werk),Ge-werb-e-schein 营业执照,ab-werb-en 挖角,an-werb-en 招募,um-werb-en 追求,Wirb-el 漩涡、脊椎(\"转的\"),wirb-eln 旋转,Wirb-el-säule 脊柱,Wirbel-wind 旋风,Wirbel-tier 脊椎动物。",
    "werben (to recruit, to advertise) comes from Proto-Germanic *hwerbaną (to turn, move about), kin to Old English hweorfan (to turn, go), Dutch werven, Gothic hvairban (to walk); PIE *kʷerp- (to turn), with Greek karpos (the wrist — where turning happens). Medieval werben was to bustle about — for soldiers, a bride, custom — whence the three modern senses: Werb-ung advertising (20th century, from soliciting), courtship (um jemanden werben), recruitment (An-werb-ung), Werb-e-spot a commercial, Werb-e-agentur an ad agency, Be-werb-ung an application (bustling on one's own behalf), sich be-werb-en to apply, Be-werb-er an applicant, Wett-be-werb competition (bustling in rivalry), Wett-be-werb-er a competitor, Er-werb acquisition, a livelihood (what the bustling wins), er-werb-en to acquire, er-werbs-los unemployed, er-werbs-tätig employed, Ge-werb-e a trade (a means of living), ge-werb-lich commercial, Ge-werb-e-schein a trade licence, ab-werb-en to poach, an-werb-en to recruit, um-werb-en to woo, Wirb-el a whirl, a vertebra (the turning thing), wirb-eln to whirl, Wirb-el-säule the spine, Wirbel-wind a whirlwind, Wirbel-tier a vertebrate."],
});
